Strategy pattern:
=> The Strategy Design Pattern is a "behavioral design pattern" that allows you to define a family of algorithms (strategies), place each one in a separate class, and make them interchangeable at runtime.

Ex:
public class Program
{
	public static void Main(string[] args)
	{
		PaymentStratigy  stratigy = new PaymentStratigy(new CardPayment());
		stratigy.DoPayment();							//output: CardPayment
		stratigy.SetObject(new CoupenPayment());
		stratigy.DoPayment();							//output: CoupenPayment
	}
}

public interface IPayment
{
	void MakePayment();
}

public class CardPayment:IPayment{
	public void MakePayment(){
		Console.WriteLine("CardPayment");
	}
}
public class CoupenPayment:IPayment{
	public void MakePayment(){
		Console.WriteLine("CoupenPayment");
	}
}

public class PaymentStratigy
{
	private IPayment PaymentObj;
	public PaymentStratigy(IPayment Payment){
		this.PaymentObj = Payment;
	}

	public void SetObject(IPayment Payment){	//Setter for the object
		this.PaymentObj = Payment;
	}

	public void DoPayment()
	{
		PaymentObj.MakePayment();
		SendInvoice();		//Extra method that happens after payment
	}
}

=> In the above example CardPayment, CoupenPayment are set of strategies and  PaymentStratigy is the context for the process, it is responsible for to group the strategies.
=> Here if any other payment option came means we just need to implement the IPayment interface and implement the logic and call in the main method.
=> Here the context class(PaymentStratigy) is responsible for flow execution this is the advantage of strategy pattern where we need a set of strategies to execute then we have to use it.


Template design pattern:
-> The Template Method Design Pattern is a behavioral design pattern that defines the overall structure (skeleton) of an algorithm in a base class. It allows subclasses to redefine or customize specific steps of the algorithm without changing its core structure.
-> Template design Pattern is a "behavioral design pattern", Make the process flow(template) in the parent class so that all the child classes will going to follow the same flow.
-> Suppose there is a parent class that contains f1,f2,f3 methods and some child classes inherited it and we need to control the flow (like f3 first then f1 and f2), then we going to create a processor method that contains the flow which is responsible for flow execution. This processor method need to create in parent only.

Ex: 
public class Program
{
	public static void Main(string[] args)
	{
		MakeBavarage tea = new CofeeBavarage();
		tea.BavarageProcessor();
	}
}

public abstract class MakeBavarage
{
	//Common method
	public void BoilWater()
	{
		Console.WriteLine("Boil Water");
	}
	//Individual method
	public abstract void Brew();

	public void PourInCup()
	{
		Console.WriteLine("Pour In Cup");
	}

	public abstract void AddCordiments();

	//Actual template method
	private void BavarageProcessor(){
		BoilWater();
		Brew();
		PourInCup();
		AddCordiments();
	}
}

public class TeaBavarage : MakeBavarage
{
	public override void Brew()
	{
		Console.WriteLine("Tea Brew");
	}
	public override void AddCordiments()
	{
		Console.WriteLine("Tea Cordiments");
	}
}

public class CofeeBavarage: MakeBavarage
{
	public override void Brew()
	{
		Console.WriteLine("Cofee Brew");
	}
	public override void AddCordiments()
	{
		Console.WriteLine("Cofee Cordiments");
	}
}

=> Here the flow is same for both the processes but there is a change in the Brew and Cordiments. This is template design pattern.


Builder Design Pattern:
=> The Builder Design Pattern is a creational design pattern that provides a step-by-step approach to constructing complex objects.
=> Suppose there is a situation to create object of the class that accepts parameters, but when creating the object we need to remember the order of the parameters and no of parameters. Suppose there is a change in order we need to change all the objects which is huge thing. We can also give setters to set the values but some values may misses, to overcome the situation builder pattern come.
Ex:
public class Program
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        UserProfile profile = new UserProfile.Builder().setName("Sai").setId(1).Build();	//object creation
    }
}

public class UserProfile
{
    private int Id;
    private string Name;
    private UserProfile(Builder builder)
    {
        this.Id = builder.Id;
        this.Name = builder.Name;
    }

    public class Builder
    {
        public int Id;
        public string Name;
        public Builder setId(int Id)
        {
            this.Id = Id;
            return this;
        }
        public Builder setName(string Name)
        {
            this.Name = Name;
            return this;
        }
        public UserProfile Build()
        {
            return new UserProfile(this);
        }
    }
    
}

State Design Pattern:
=> The State Design Pattern is a behavioral design pattern that lets an object alter its behavior when its internal state changes. This avoids complex conditional statements and makes the system more maintainable and flexible.

Ex: Media player can have different states like play, pause and stop where 
1. play is done when the state not in play or stop.
2. pause is done when the state not in pause or stop.
3. stop is done when the state not in stop.

class Program
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        MediaPlayer player = new MediaPlayer();
        player.Play();
        player.Stop();
        player.Play();
        player.Pause();
        Console.ReadLine();
    }
}

public class MediaPlayer
{
    private IState _state = new StopState();

    public void SetState(IState state)
    {
        _state = state;
    }
    public void Play()
    {
        _state.Play(this);
    }
    public void Pause()
    {
        _state.Pause(this);
    }
    public void Stop()
    {
        _state.Stop(this);
    }
}

public interface IState
{
    void Play(MediaPlayer mediaPlayer);
    void Pause(MediaPlayer mediaPlayer);
    void Stop(MediaPlayer mediaPlayer);
}

 public class PlayState : IState
 {
     public void Pause(MediaPlayer mediaPlayer)
     {
         Console.WriteLine("Pausing media playback.");
         mediaPlayer.SetState(new PlayState());
     }

     public void Play(MediaPlayer mediaPlayer)
     {
         Console.WriteLine("Media is already playing.");
     }

     public void Stop(MediaPlayer mediaPlayer)
     {
         Console.WriteLine("Stopping media playback.");
         mediaPlayer.SetState(new StopState());
     }
 }

 public class PauseState: IState
 {
     public void Pause(MediaPlayer mediaPlayer)
     {
         Console.WriteLine("Media is already paused.");
     }
     public void Play(MediaPlayer mediaPlayer)
     {
         Console.WriteLine("Resuming media playback.");
         mediaPlayer.SetState(new PlayState());
     }
     public void Stop(MediaPlayer mediaPlayer)
     {
         Console.WriteLine("Stopping media playback.");
         mediaPlayer.SetState(new StopState());
     }
 }

public class StopState : IState
{
    public void Pause(MediaPlayer mediaPlayer)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Media is stop state");
    }

    public void Play(MediaPlayer mediaPlayer)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Playing media playback.");
        mediaPlayer.SetState(new PlayState());
    }

    public void Stop(MediaPlayer mediaPlayer)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Already is stop state");
    }
}

